0.8.0 "Trust, but Verify" (20260804)

	Feature-preserving hardening release. HTML output now has one safe
	escaping boundary; mutating actions default to dry-run and use atomic,
	metadata-preserving replacement; dependency decisions share an EAPI 8
	matcher for versions, slots, repositories, and USE dependencies; and
	effective USE/virtual resolution covers the command-used semantics.
	Explicit root variables and whitespace-bearing CONTENTS paths are fixed.
	A test-only Python Portage oracle gates hundreds of installed/visible
	atom decisions, and release archives must carry the exact source test
	inventory. No command or feature was removed.

	* fix: preserve USE-qualified dependency atoms through reverse-dependency
	  discovery and formatting. The shared matcher now applies version, slot,
	  repository, and USE constraints consistently to dependency metadata,
	  masks, package.use, package.accept_keywords, and CLI queries.
	* fix: mirror Portage's effective USE precedence across active profiles,
	  repositories and their masters, package rules, IUSE defaults, FEATURES,
	  and environment overrides. Multi-flag records are normalized before
	  stacking so removals and later overrides remain effective.
	* fix: reject invalid EROOT, EPREFIX, and PORTAGE_CONFIGROOT values before
	  dispatch. A standalone explicit EPREFIX now obtains its matching EROOT
	  from Portage instead of combining Prefix configuration with host paths.
	* fix: make configuration replacement same-directory and atomic, preserve
	  available metadata and valid in-root symlinks, reject unsafe symlinks,
	  validate staged content, and report the underlying write failure.
	* fix: build virtual provider maps only from package- and keyword-visible
	  ebuilds while retaining providers contributed by every visible version.
	* fix: tighten slot/repository suffix recognition, consolidate repository
	  discovery, and keep plain HTML escaping separate from ANSI/OSC
	  translation. Carriage returns and inherited renderer markers can no
	  longer bypass the HTML boundary.
	* fix: harden value and predicate memoisation against redundant directory
	  creation, oversized keys, and empty or malformed cached statuses.
	* packaging: avoid pulling zsh solely for completion files, keep the live
	  Portage oracle out of minimal-chroot src_test, and compare archives with
	  the tracked test inventory rather than scratch files.
	* tests: expand the Python Portage oracle across operators, cross-package
	  mismatches, USE defaults and metadata lookups; isolate normal test loads
	  from parser argv; and add focused regression coverage for every audited
	  path above.
	* docs: align license metadata with the GPLv3-only source grant; correct the
	  generated manual's action, world-path, and merge wording; document every
	  verification tier; and keep generated roff warning-clean under mandoc.

0.7.5 "Same Difference" (20260609)

	Patch release.  More fixes from the per-flag shakeout — a
	missing-ChangeLog error leak, a trailing-comment / comments-only
	data-loss in `-E`, a metadata-cache gap on cache-less overlays, and
	signal- and idle-exit behaviour — plus a new property-invariant test
	tier.  The codename is the invariant that tier asserts: apply a
	config-mutating action twice and the second run makes no difference.

	* fix: src/dep — info_action_changelog (`-j` / `--changelog`) printed
	  a "From …/ChangeLog:" header and then leaked a raw "No such file"
	  for every package: modern trees ship no per-package ChangeLog and
	  the `done <…/ChangeLog` redirect was unguarded.  It now checks the
	  file exists first and emits a clean "No ChangeLog available for
	  <cp>" notice instead.
	* fix: src/dep — filter_etc_file (`-E`) dropped a trailing comment
	  block, and truncated a comments-only file to zero bytes, because a
	  pending comment block was flushed only on a blank line or a kept
	  entry — never at EOF.  As `-E` defaults to force, that was silent
	  data loss; the loop now flushes any pending block at EOF.
	* fix: src/dep — extract_var read package metadata only from a repo's
	  in-tree metadata/md5-cache (and the vardb), never Portage's depcache
	  under $EDB_DIR/dep.  Overlays that ship no in-tree cache — so
	  Portage generated it into /var/cache/edb — failed every metadata op
	  on their uninstalled packages (`dep -O` alone logged 79 "failed
	  extract_var").  The depcache is the identical key=value format and
	  is now an EROOT-aware fallback, cutting that overlay's failures to 3
	  (ebuilds Portage itself has not yet cached, so no metadata exists in
	  any cache to read).
	* fix: src/dep — the cleanup trap was a shared `rm -rf` on EXIT and
	  TERM with no exit, so a SIGTERM or timeout(1) mid-run deleted the
	  temp dir and let the script run on, spamming "No such file" (once
	  for a killed `-wp`, 700+ times for the recursive `-s`).  It now
	  splits into a one-time EXIT cleanup and a `TERM → exit 143` trap
	  (mirroring the existing SIGINT `exit 1`).  Separately, `dep -s`
	  (spring-clean) returned 1 when there was nothing to do — makeideal's
	  trailing `[[ "$missing" ]] && …` leaked out as the exit code; it now
	  returns 0 when idle.
	* tests: the fixes above add 8 regression unit tests (257 → 265),
	  each verified load-bearing against the pre-fix code.  Plus a new
	  property-invariant tier (tests/property/, `make check-properties`,
	  11 tests) that drives the real filter_etc_portage / filter_world
	  over synthetic fixtures and asserts oracle-free invariants —
	  idempotence, atom-inventory subset, no duplicate flag, comment
	  preservation — the transform-corruption class the stubbed unit tier
	  and the read-only smoke tier both miss.  Wired into CI; a gitignored
	  scripts/verify-local.sh runs the same checks over a copy of the real
	  /etc/portage.

	AI-assist disclosure as for 0.7.1.

0.7.4 "Mind the EROOT" (20260604)

	Patch release.  Four fixes to the config-file apply pipeline
	(`dep -E` / `dep -w`), all variations on one theme: honour the
	target environment instead of a hardcoded path, the system root,
	or a stale assumption.  Surfaced by a sandbox-isolated re-run of
	the mutating actions against a non-root, non-`/` EROOT.

	* fix: src/dep — ask_install_new_file's `pretend` case called
	  `exit 0` instead of `return 0`.  Under `dep -E --pretend` the
	  first config file with redundant entries ended the entire
	  filter_etc_portage walk, so every later file (package.mask,
	  package.accept_keywords, …) was silently never previewed.  It
	  now returns and the walk continues.  Fires only when a file
	  actually has redundant entries — the upstream `diff -q && return`
	  guard means a clean tree never reached it, which is why it went
	  unnoticed.
	* fix: src/dep — filter_world passed a hardcoded
	  /var/lib/portage/world as the install target instead of
	  $WORLD_FILE.  On a Prefix / non-default EROOT install, `dep -w`
	  in apply mode would rewrite the host's real world file rather
	  than the configured one.  It now uses $WORLD_FILE, and the
	  filter_world prose is EROOT-aware.
	* fix: src/dep — install_new_file always escalated (doas/sudo/su)
	  when non-root, even when the target was already the caller's to
	  write (an existing writable file, or a new file in a writable
	  directory).  This produced spurious privilege prompts and
	  root-owned files when dep runs against a user-owned tree (a
	  non-root EROOT, a test sandbox).  It now writes directly when
	  the target is writable and escalates only when it genuinely
	  isn't.
	* fix: src/dep — filter_etc_file did not clear a pending comment
	  block when the entry it introduced was dropped, so the dropped
	  entry's comment migrated onto the next kept entry (when no blank
	  line separated them).  The comment is now discarded together
	  with the entry it introduced.
	* tests: 7 new unit tests (250 → 257) pin all four fixes
	  (tests/unit/ask_install_new_file.bats, filter_world.bats,
	  install_new_file.bats, filter_etc_file.bats), each verified
	  load-bearing by running it against the pre-fix code.  The three
	  apply-path fixes were additionally validated end-to-end against
	  a writable sandbox, with the live tree confirmed byte-identical
	  afterward.

	AI-assist disclosure as for 0.7.1.

0.7.3 "Truth in Advertising" (20260531)

	Twin patch release with portconf 2.0.1.  The headline fixes are
	one stdin-clobbered prompt class (in portconf) and one mis-scoped
	vardb scan (in udept's db_grep); both surfaced through a critical
	re-read of the recently-added one-line docstrings and section
	banners.

	* fix: src/dep — db_grep's default branch (active when neither
	  --original-depends nor --uninstalled is set) hardcoded the
	  *DEPEND file set and ignored $attr.  potential_revdepends
	  calls it with attr='*DEPEND' so that worked; info_action_iuse
	  calls it with attr=IUSE expecting an IUSE-scoped scan and
	  silently got a *DEPEND-scoped one instead.  The follow-up
	  `has $1 $(extract_var IUSE …)` filter trims false positives
	  but not false negatives — packages whose USE flag is in IUSE
	  but doesn't affect deps were silently missed by `dep -U
	  <flag>`.  $attr is now honoured: pseudo-name "*DEPEND"
	  expands to the five dep variants so potential_revdepends
	  behaviour is preserved; empty $attr falls back to the same
	  expansion.  Commit baa8baf.
	* tests: tests/unit/db_grep.bats — 5 new tests pinning attr-
	  honouring behaviour, including the false-negative case that
	  motivated the fix.

	Post-audit accuracy pass on docstrings (comment-only, behaviour
	unchanged):

	* src/dep: 13 docstrings corrected for factual drift.  No
	  inversions or completely-wrong descriptions were found —
	  the failure mode here is omission (missing branch, missing
	  flag, missing fallback) rather than the direction-inversions
	  the parallel portconf audit surfaced.  Affected functions
	  include provided_and_avail (was "installed mlsrs"; actually
	  injected+installed), info_action_depstrings (default omits
	  DEPEND/BDEPEND unless --build-time), info_action_info (omitted
	  RESTRICT + DESCRIPTION), filter_etc_portage (omitted legacy
	  package.keywords), etc_portage_files (hardcoded "/etc/portage";
	  actually Prefix-aware), and others.

	Pre-existing 0.7.3-seed commits already on origin/main:

	* fix: env.d USE-resolution silent on missing profile.env;
	  smoke tests now stage3-tolerant.  Commit 355136a.
	* fix: zsh completion — `_dep "$@"` emitted AFTER the option-
	  table dump so first-invocation completion actually fires.
	  Commit df64644.
	* ci: functional-completion check — autoload + invoke +
	  flag-count drift detection.  Commit e347274.
	* src/dep: 24 topical section banners + `make toc` target
	  + CONTRIBUTING.md "Source layout" note.  Commit ac7efc9.
	* src/dep: one-line docstrings above every function (~150).
	  The ones the re-read flagged as drifty are corrected in
	  baa8baf above.  Commit c9447aa.
	* tests/smoke: `-L` rev-depends tests 17 + 18 + 21 now accept
	  exit 124 from /usr/bin/timeout alongside 0 — populated
	  maintainer systems exceed the 120s cap; CI stage3 doesn't.
	  Commit e0c2a1e.
	* docs: SECURITY.md disclosure policy (5 sections: supported
	  versions, PVR reporting, in-scope, out-of-scope, disclosure).
	  Commit e267647.
	* docs: README — latest-release shields.io badge next to CI.
	  Commit 3af6011.

	Build:

	* configure.ac: AC_INIT 0.7.2 → 0.7.3, PACKAGE_TAG
	  "The Linter Was Right" → "Truth in Advertising".

0.7.2 "The Linter Was Right" (20260516)

	* fix: comm_ver rc-suffix mis-ordering (commit d721e8e). The
	  status-class numeric mapping used a length-based formula
	  that placed rc at scn=6 (highest), contradicting both the
	  function's own header docstring (rc=3, between pre and
	  release) and Portage's `alpha < beta < pre < rc < release
	  < p` specification. Effect: every vercmp comparison
	  involving an rc release returned the wrong answer —
	  `1.0_rc < 1.0` was FALSE in vercmp; downstream dep_satisfies,
	  slot resolution, and the dep walker would mis-rank rc
	  releases. Replaced with an explicit case statement that
	  matches the docstring and spec.
	* fix: comm_ver `1.0` vs `1.0-r0` aborted via exit 128 (also
	  in d721e8e). r0 is the implicit-default revision; the
	  /#-r*(0) parameter expansion normalises both `1.0` and
	  `1.0-r0` to empty s_r. At the function's final revision
	  compare, both -lt and -gt fail on empty operands (bash
	  treats "" as 0 in arithmetic context), so the function
	  reached its `Error! ... exit 128` fall-through and halted
	  the entire script. Fixed with an explicit equality check
	  before the inequality compares.
	* tests: +5 unit tests in tests/unit/comm_ver.bats covering
	  all four rc-ordering boundaries (pre < rc, rc < release,
	  rc < p, 1.0_rc literal) plus 1.0 == 1.0-r0 commutativity.
	* both bugs were surfaced (but deferred) during the 0.7.1
	  test-pinning pass; see the 0.7.1 ChangeLog entry's
	  "known bugs (deferred to 0.7.2)" subsection for context.
	* tests: load_dep — save/restore bats EXIT / TERM / ERR
	  traps so failing assertions register correctly (commit
	  4e200da). Pre-fix, dep.in's top-level trap installation
	  (line ~558) clobbered bats's `bats_exit_trap` used for
	  result reporting, and dep.in's non-zero top-level lines
	  fired bats's `bats_error_trap` mid-source — together,
	  these caused FAILING bats assertions to silently drop
	  from the TAP report (the test was counted in 1..N but
	  neither `ok` nor `not ok` was emitted; bats reported
	  `Executed M instead of expected N tests` and exited 1).
	  Bisected to the root cause, fixed in test_helper.bash
	  with explicit `trap -p ... + eval` save/restore. Re-
	  enabled 2 negative tests in tests/unit/usecomponent.bats
	  that had been dropped because of this limitation.
	* comm_ver: defensive hardening on unknown status suffix
	  (commit fdb30bd). The case statement's `*)` catch-all
	  for an unrecognised suffix-category previously mapped to
	  scn=99 (sorting it above every known class). Replaced
	  with `format_error + return 128`, so a malformed cpv
	  surfaces on stderr instead of silently miscomparing.
	  Path is unreachable from normal callers (the suffix
	  parser only accepts the literal set _alpha|beta|pre|rc|p)
	  but the catch-all defends future parser extensions.
	* tests: +7 unit tests in tests/unit/comm_ver.bats covering
	  edge cases the audit hadn't pinned — status number ordering
	  (alpha1 < alpha2, alpha10 > alpha9 numerically, alpha01
	  == alpha1 leading-zero), letter+status precedence (1.2a_
	  beta < 1.2b_alpha), cvs.* snapshot ordering, bare-vs-
	  numbered suffix (1.0_pre < 1.0_pre1).
	* tests: usecomponent env.d missing-file test uses
	  `run --separate-stderr` (bats >=1.5) instead of a sed-
	  wording-coupled $output assertion (commit a9a74d3).
	* ebuild: BDEPEND test dependency tightened from
	  `dev-util/bats` to `>=dev-util/bats-1.5.0` to match the
	  --separate-stderr requirement above. Portage's current
	  bats is 1.13.0, well above the floor; the bump enforces
	  the minimum for older stage3-style setups.
	* docs: 0.7.1 ChangeLog rewording — replaced specific line
	  numbers in the bug-fix and known-bug bullets with topic
	  descriptions that survive subsequent edits (commit 16cd7ea).
	* Suite 231 (start of 0.7.2 scope) -> 245 (end).
	* AI-assisted: the 0.7.2 work, like 0.7.1 / 0.7.0 / 0.6.0,
	  used Claude (Anthropic) as a coding assistant. Every
	  change reviewed by hand and validated against the bats
	  unit + smoke tiers plus `make distcheck`.

0.7.1 "Skeletons in the Cache" (20260516)

	* fix: USE-resolution case-shadow (commit 8673115). The
	  pkginternal and env.d cases in usecomponent() had empty
	  placeholder branches above the real handlers, silently
	  shadowing the dispatch (bash case takes the first match).
	  Because the default USE_ORDER is
	  'env:pkg:conf:defaults:pkginternal:env.d', every call to
	  dbuse() walked through both shadowed tiers and produced
	  nothing — udept had been doing USE resolution missing two
	  of its six tiers since the modern-era refactor.
	  pkginternal_use_for() was completely unreachable.
	* fix: comm_ver typo s1_s/s2_s (commit 8aa2c90). The
	  length-difference short-circuit (the ${s1_l}${...}${s1_r}
	  concat check after the main-array loop) referenced
	  undefined variables ${s1_s} / ${s2_s} (typo for ${s1_ss} /
	  ${s2_ss}). They expanded empty, so the comparison reduced
	  to letter+revision only and skipped the status suffix.
	  Effect: version pairs like 1.2_alpha1 vs 1.2.0_beta2
	  returned equal — wrong.
	* fix: virtual_version error mask (in commit 187de46).
	  `local virtual_mlsr=$(virtual_version ...) || return` never
	  fired the `|| return` clause because `local` always returns
	  0 (the assignment-binding's success, not the inner command's
	  exit). Failure of virtual_version was silently swallowed.
	  Split decl from assign so the chain works.
	* shellcheck: src/dep is now clean at --severity=error (CI
	  gate) AND --severity=warning (down from 92 findings). The
	  two intentional patterns kept as inline disables with
	  rationale (SC2104 on the function-loop `continue` in
	  handle_arg_info, SC2151 on the signed-magnitude negative-
	  return convention in comm_float / comm_ver). The systemic
	  word-splitting style suppressed in .shellcheckrc with
	  per-rule explanation (SC2046 for `$(...)` lists fed to
	  commands as separate args, SC2206 for whitespace-split
	  array assignments, SC2229 for indirect `read -a "$return"`
	  array assignment).
	* build: CI switched from `make dist` to `make distcheck`
	  (caught and fixed two srcdir/builddir bugs in doc/Makefile.am
	  and completion/Makefile.am that pointed at $(top_srcdir)/
	  src/dep for a generated file; broke under distcheck's split
	  layout but invisible in-tree).
	* tests: bats smoke tier introduced at tests/smoke/
	  (test_helper.bash + 7 .bats files, 41 tests across help /
	  info_actions / virtuals / existence / output_modes /
	  errors / actions). Each test asserts exit status plus one
	  stable structural marker. Reverse-walk tests accept timeout
	  (exit 124) as a valid outcome alongside 0 on populated
	  maintainer systems. `make check-smoke` target added.
	* tests: +31 new unit tests pinning the bug fixes —
	  tests/unit/comm_ver.bats (20) covers the s1_ss typo
	  regression case + portage convention + status-class
	  ordering + revision + letter suffix + vercmp wrapper
	  contracts. tests/unit/usecomponent.bats (11) covers the
	  case-shadow regression pin + pkginternal_use_for direct
	  unit + env.d profile.env extraction. Total unit suite:
	  200 -> 231.
	* CI: tests/smoke.sh + tests/smoke-diff.sh + tests/baseline.ci
	  remain in tree for local snapshot-diff work
	  (`make smoke / smoke-baseline / smoke-diff`) but are no
	  longer CI-gating — `make check-smoke` replaces them as the
	  CI tier.
	* docs: CONTRIBUTING.md, .editorconfig, .shellcheckrc, and
	  .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/{bug_report,feature_request,config}
	  ported from portconf's project-hygiene set, adapted for
	  udept's actual tool surface (portageq + emerge, not eix +
	  qatom).
	* known bugs (deferred to 0.7.2): two more vercmp issues
	  surfaced during the test-pinning pass — (a) rc-suffix
	  mis-ordering: the status-class numeric mapping in the
	  suffix loop produces rc=6 (highest), contradicting the
	  function's own docstring (rc=3, between pre and release)
	  and Portage's spec. (b) `1.0` vs `1.0-r0` aborts the script
	  via exit 128 because the empty-empty rev case falls through
	  both `-lt` and `-gt` to the function's error-exit path.
	  Documented in tests/unit/comm_ver.bats header; tests for
	  both are omitted from this release to keep the suite green.
	  Both fixed in 0.7.2 immediately following this release;
	  see the 0.7.2 ChangeLog entry above.
	* AI-assisted: the 0.7.1 work, like 0.7.0 and 0.6.0, used
	  Claude (Anthropic) as a coding assistant. Every change
	  reviewed by hand and validated against the bats unit and
	  smoke tiers plus `make distcheck`.

0.7.0 "Patched All The Way Down" (20260509)

	* slot-aware revdep filtering: 'dep -L cat/pkg-version' filters
	  reverse-dependencies to those satisfied by the installed slot.
	  Slot info now appears in revdep output. 'dep -L cat/pkg' (no
	  version) stays slot-agnostic. -X and -T inherit the same
	  matcher transitively.
	* add --required-use (-Q) info action: read REQUIRED_USE from
	  md5-cache, evaluate against active USE (or vardb USE under
	  --original-depends), report unsatisfied clauses. Process
	  exits non-zero when any package fails its REQUIRED_USE.
	* add --full-atoms flag: with --for-emerge, append :slot::repo
	  to emitted atoms ('=cat/pkg-1.0:0::gentoo'). Sub-slots
	  stripped.
	* fix --for-emerge consistency in format_revdep, rtnode, and
	  fuzzy-filename owners-match — '=' prefix no longer dropped.
	* zsh completion: native '_dep' file alongside the bash one,
	  generated from the same option-table source. New
	  --enable-zsh-completion configure option, default on.
	* ebuild moves to EAPI 8 with modern SRC_URI, completion
	  toggles (+bash-completion / +zsh-completion), +test USE
	  running the bats suite via src_test(), RDEPEND on
	  bash >= 4.2 / portage >= 2.3.
	* test infrastructure: 200-test bats unit suite covering
	  dep_satisfies, dep_to_cps, world_sets_expand, the BNF DEPEND
	  parser, the slot matchers, the REQUIRED_USE evaluator, and
	  the format/resolve helpers. Smoke harness in tests/smoke.sh
	  with tolerance-aware diff helper. 'make check / smoke /
	  smoke-baseline / smoke-diff' targets. GitHub Actions CI runs
	  both layers plus a regression check against a checked-in
	  stage3 baseline.
	* AI-assisted: the 0.7.0 work, like 0.6.0, used Claude
	  (Anthropic) as a coding assistant. Every change was reviewed
	  by hand and validated against the bats and smoke layers.

0.6.0 "The Treadmill Was Always Going To Win" (20260507)

	* first modernization release in ~20 years; based on
	  init-6/udept on GitLab
	* read repository configuration from /etc/portage/repos.conf
	* read ebuild metadata from metadata/md5-cache (no more
	  /var/cache/edb/dep regen via ebuild.sh)
	* parse modern atom syntax: slot dependencies, sub-slots,
	  USE-dependencies, IUSE prefix characters (+, -)
	* handle BDEPEND and IDEPEND alongside DEPEND/RDEPEND/PDEPEND
	* resolve virtuals against the virtual/* package category (the
	  PROVIDE-based model is gone in EAPI 5+)
	* understand modern overlay layout for --overlay-clean
	  (Manifest2, metadata/layout.conf)
	* expand world sets (@<set> entries in
	  /var/lib/portage/world_sets and /etc/portage/sets/)
	* use 'portageq envvar -v' for environment resolution instead
	  of an in-script make.conf shell-lexer
	* support Gentoo Prefix and non-'/' EROOT installs

